          "source": "def-knot-a-polynomial",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume full AC and let $K\\subset S^3$ be a smooth oriented knot with exterior $E_K$, positive meridian $\\mu$, and preferred longitude $\\lambda$. Define $\\mathcal E_K\\subset(\\mathbb C^*)^2$ to consist of all pairs $(M,L)$ for which some representation $\\rho:\\pi_1(E_K)\\to SL_2(\\mathbb C)$ has a common invariant line on which $\\rho(\\mu)$ acts by $M$ and $\\rho(\\lambda)$ acts by $L$. The Zariski closure is the smallest Zariski-closed algebraic subset containing $\\mathcal E_K$; let $V_K$ be the union of its one-dimensional irreducible components, where an irreducible component is a maximal irreducible closed subset. Zero-dimensional image components are omitted. The **ordinary A-polynomial** $A_K(M,L)$ is the Laurent polynomial generating the vanishing ideal of $V_K$, chosen square-free (no repeated irreducible factors) and with primitive integer coefficients (their greatest common divisor is one). It is determined up to multiplication by $\\pm M^aL^b$ for $a,b\\in\\mathbb Z$. Its reduced curve includes the abelian component $L=1$; equivalently, its representative in $\\mathbb Z[M,L]$ is divisible by $L-1$.",
