          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-knot-a-polynomial-boundary-elimination",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume full AC and let $K\\subset S^3$ be a smooth oriented knot. The restriction of representations of $\\pi_1(E_K)$ to the peripheral torus gives an algebraic eigenvalue locus in $(\\mathbb C^*)^2$. Its one-dimensional reduced image components are exactly the curve components used in $A_K(M,L)$; isolated image points contribute no curve factor. Eliminating the representation and trace coordinates from the finite presentation equations yields the Laurent ideal of that curve union, whose square-free primitive generator is $A_K$ up to $\\pm M^aL^b$.",
          "uses": []
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-boundary-slope-of-an-incompressible-surface",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume full AC. Let $K\\subset S^3$ be a smooth oriented knot with chosen compact exterior $E_K$ and boundary torus $T_K=\\partial E_K$. A simple closed curve on a surface is **essential** if it is not null-homotopic there. A surface $S\\subset E_K$ is properly embedded if $S\\cap\\partial E_K=\\partial S$ and its interior lies in the interior of $E_K$. It is **incompressible** if it has no embedded disk with interior disjoint from $S$ and essential boundary in $S$; it is **boundary-incompressible** if it has no embedded disk whose boundary is the union of an arc in $S$ that does not cut off a disk and an arc in $\\partial E_K$. A **boundary slope** of $K$ is the unoriented isotopy class of an essential simple closed curve on $T_K$ that occurs as the boundary of a properly embedded, orientable, incompressible, boundary-incompressible surface. Using the positive meridian $\\mu$ and preferred longitude $\\lambda$ as a homology basis, a primitive class $a[\\lambda]+b[\\mu]$ has slope $b/a\\in\\mathbb Q\\cup\\{\\infty\\}$, with $a=0$ interpreted as $\\infty$. A boundary slope is **strict** if no realizing surface is a fiber of a locally trivial fibration $E_K\\to S^1$; a fiber is the inverse image of a point under that bundle projection.",
          "uses": []
        },
