        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-coprime-torus-knot",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. Identify the standard oriented three-sphere with $S^3=\\{(z_1,z_2)\\in\\mathbb C^2:|z_1|^2+|z_2|^2=1\\}$ and choose $0<r<1$. The standard unknotted Heegaard torus is $T_r=\\{|z_1|=r,\\ |z_2|=\\sqrt{1-r^2}\\}$; its two sides are solid tori. In the angular coordinates $(u,v)\\in(\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z)^2$ given by $(u,v)\\mapsto(r e^{2\\pi i u},\\sqrt{1-r^2}e^{2\\pi i v})$, let $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ be the first and second coordinate classes in $H_1(T_r;\\mathbb Z)$. For coprime nonzero integers $p,q$, the **oriented torus knot** $T(p,q)$ is the image of $s\\mapsto(r e^{2\\pi i p s},\\sqrt{1-r^2}e^{2\\pi i q s})$, for $s\\in\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z$, oriented by increasing $s$; it represents the primitive slope $p\\alpha+q\\beta$. The mirror convention is the reflection $(z_1,z_2)\\mapsto(z_1,\\overline{z_2})$, which sends $T(p,q)$ to $T(p,-q)$ with image orientation, and reversing the knot orientation gives $T(-p,-q)$. For nonzero $p,q$ with $d=\\gcd(|p|,|q|)>1$, the corresponding torus link is the union of $d$ disjoint parallel copies of the primitive slope $(p/d,q/d)$; it is not a single embedded curve of class $p\\alpha+q\\beta$. The exterior is $E_K=S^3\\setminus\\operatorname{int}N(K)$ for a chosen closed tubular neighborhood. A positive meridian is the oriented boundary of a normal disk, and a preferred longitude is a degree-one class on $\\partial E_K$ whose image in $H_1(E_K;\\mathbb Z)$ is zero; this definition alone asserts neither existence nor uniqueness.",
